 "Early March in Mexico City is the tail of the dry season and about as good as it gets: highs 25-27C, lows 10-12C, almost no rain, very strong UV at 2,240 m. Two real caveats. First, it is cold at night and in the shade - bring a jacket, and note that several heritage hotels including Casa Decu have no heating. Second, March is the worst month of the year for air quality: thermal inversions trap pollution and CDMX issues ozone contingencies most often in March and April. If anyone in the group is asthmatic, check the index before a long day outdoors. Altitude also hits harder than people expect on day one - go easy on the mezcal the first night.": "",
 "Early March is the best weather Miami gets. Average highs rise from 78F to 81F (26-27C) across the month, average low 70F (21C), sea temperature about 76F (24C) — swimmable but not warm. March is statistically the DRIEST month: about 6 rainy days, ~53mm total, and the sky is clear or partly cloudy 68% of daylight hours. The real March variable is wind, not rain: cold fronts push down the peninsula bringing two or three days of 15-25kt northerlies that chop up the bay and can cancel boat days. Pack one warm layer for evenings on the water.": "",
 "Early March is the dry season and about as good as this coast gets: lows around 24-25C, highs 30-32C, sea at 28-29C, roughly 11.6 hours of sun and very little rain. The catch is wind. December through May the trade winds blow steadily over the Caribbean coast and March sits inside that window - it keeps the city bearable and the mosquitoes down, but it also churns the sea. Mornings are calm; the water builds through the afternoon. That is why every boat activity in this plan is scheduled before noon. March is also firmly high season (mid-January to March, at roughly 1.5-2x low-season rates), so prices are at their annual peak. One piece of timing luck: Semana Santa 2027 opens on Palm Sunday, 21 March, so an early-March trip sits clear of the single most expensive week on this coast. Do not let the dates drift past mid-March.": "",
 "Early March is the tail of Lima's summer and the best weather of the year: highs around 26-28C, nights 19-20C, high humidity (70-85%), strong UV, and essentially no rain - Lima is a coastal desert and the grey garua drizzle is a May-October phenomenon, not a March one. The sea is at its annual warmest but the Humboldt current still keeps it cool, roughly 20-22C, so the malecon beaches are swimmable-but-bracing rather than tropical. HONESTY: the web-search budget ran out before a Lima March climate page could be fetched, so these figures come from general climate knowledge rather than a page fetched in this pass - sanity-check on weather-and-climate.com or senamhi.gob.pe before packing.": "",
 "Early March on the Oaxaca coast is peak dry season and close to perfect. Highs 31-33C, lows 21-23C, sea around 27-28C, humidity moderate, and statistically almost zero rain - March is the driest month of the year here. The sun is brutal between 11:00 and 16:00 and the UV index runs extreme; the shade-and-siesta rhythm is not laziness, it is necessary. Surf: March sits between the big southern-hemisphere swell seasons, so Zicatela's Mexican Pipeline is working but not at its April-to-October monster size, and La Punta's left is at its most user-friendly - the best month of the year to learn. Carrizalillo is calm and swimmable throughout. March is also the tail of humpback whale season, so the dawn boat trips still see them. Mosquitoes are at their annual low in the dry season but the lagoons at dusk are another matter - take repellent for Manialtepec, and remember you cannot wear it if you are swimming in the bioluminescence.": "",
